The given relation is $x y+y^{2}=\tan x+y$. Differentiating both sides with respect to $x$, we get

$\frac{d}{d x}(x y)+\frac{d}{d x}\left(y^{2}\right)=\frac{d}{d x}(\tan x)+\frac{d y}{d x}$ or $\left[y \cdot 1+x \cdot \frac{d y}{d x}\right]+2 y \frac{d y}{d x}=\sec ^{2} x+\frac{d y}{d x}

or (x+2 y-1) \frac{d y}{d x}=\sec ^{2} x-y\therefore \frac{d y}{d x}=\frac{\sec ^{2} x-y}{(x+2 y-1)}$
